{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:15:47.944726Z","id":"94f64dc940ff620c43e5ada05050870591c70a23e2a4c79e9d8282d31f8bb0c6","body":{"detail":"No kubectl, kustomize or cluster was available: deploy/k8s/{base,sqlite,postgres,postgres-dev} were never built with kustomize nor applied. clax-fleet's tests check only that the variables they set are ones the binary reads and that base/fleet.toml parses (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs tests). Validate with `kubectl kustomize deploy/k8s/<overlay> | kubeconform -strict` and a kind cluster.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1},"end":{"line":24},"content_hash":"0c4b6e894d7aeafe5e37ff904a514eccbed1c194ff49f260ce9959745c015733"},"summary":"The manifests have not been rendered or validated against a cluster","tags":["test-coverage","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800789Z","id":"f3620c4abb15438a738985bb05d5c9099f1fa32a754d02ce800226f4011f286a","body":{"detail":"Port 7480 takes x-clax-fleet-hop and x-clax-fleet-instance from any caller (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs dispatch, open record 462bbaa8) and carries session cookies and tokens in plaintext between pods (proxy.rs forward); port 7481 serves /status with every tenant's instance name without authentication (fleet.rs admin_router). With no NetworkPolicy in base, sqlite or postgres, any pod in any namespace of the cluster can reach both, and in postgres-dev any pod can log into the database with the password committed in deploy/k8s/postgres-dev/kustomization.yaml. A NetworkPolicy is the cheapest layer that turns the hop headers' trust into 'only the Ingress controller and the fleet's own pods'.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":12},"end":{"line":15},"content_hash":"bdd2376fb58a77fe70d4e2847620b4c3435002a6f0cf9c36a26755f4005923a3"},"suggested_fix":"Add a NetworkPolicy to base allowing ingress to 7480 only from the fleet's pods and the Ingress controller's namespace (a label the operator sets), and to 7481 only from the fleet's pods (kubelet probes are not subject to it on most CNIs); add one to postgres-dev allowing 5432 only from the fleet's pods.","summary":"No NetworkPolicy limits who reaches the pods' 7480 and 7481 ports","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.215088Z","id":"5f8050199e39712a60422168a0250029cf65810daf82304e2444bd2199ef391f","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:60-61 says base/fleet.toml becomes the clax-fleet-catalog ConfigMap, and docs/kubernetes.md:50-53 already tells operators to keep an overlay of their own for the image. An operator's catalog belongs in that overlay too, which kustomize supports with a configMapGenerator entry of the same name and `behavior: replace` (https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/references/kustomize/kustomization/configmapgenerator/), keeping disableNameSuffixHash so pods are not restarted. Without it, upgrading the repository conflicts with local edits to base files.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":16},"end":{"line":24},"content_hash":"ac2002d7df93bb7591c0f98bbe0ba13bfdd5bd250b7bfb2d1f38bbec1d99d73a"},"suggested_fix":"Add to docs/kubernetes.md an example overlay (resources: [github.com/empathic/clax//deploy/k8s/postgres?ref=<tag>], images:, configMapGenerator: [{name: clax-fleet-catalog, behavior: replace, files: [fleet.toml], options: {disableNameSuffixHash: true}}]) and present it as the way to deploy.","summary":"Supplying one's own catalog means editing deploy/k8s/base/fleet.toml in the repository; the docs show no overlay that replaces it","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.293897Z","id":"cf33fc4f624fa0e7152c34099d471abb33482baa831634ce4ec3e6d16fb79e32","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml:12-15 lists serviceaccount, service and pdb only; grep -rln NetworkPolicy deploy docs matches only deploy/k8s/base/.qual. crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs dispatch (~754-769) picks the instance from x-clax-fleet-instance whenever the hop header is >0; admin_router (fleet.rs:715-742) serves /status with no auth; deploy/k8s/postgres-dev/kustomization.yaml:19 commits POSTGRES_PASSWORD=clax-dev.","kind":"comment","references":"f3620c4abb15438a738985bb05d5c9099f1fa32a754d02ce800226f4011f286a","summary":"confirmed: no NetworkPolicy exists in any overlay","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:21.162997Z","id":"1424e4aa8feca5fd6fca6589ec822f69dc4cc306fc4054b7b8476bb48362609c","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml:16-24 generates clax-fleet-catalog from base/fleet.toml with disableNameSuffixHash. docs/kubernetes.md:50-53 recommends an overlay of one's own for the image, and 60-61 says base/fleet.toml becomes the ConfigMap, with no example of replacing it. kustomize supports behavior: replace on a generator of the same name (https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/references/kustomize/kustomization/configmapgenerator/). Suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"5f8050199e39712a60422168a0250029cf65810daf82304e2444bd2199ef391f","summary":"confirmed: the catalog lives in base/fleet.toml with no documented overlay to replace it","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.519597Z","id":"fb0b560131195ede7b36bf0cc2fa31d946b9caf16367bb558f11b4ad7c9fab4f","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml admits 7480 from the fleet's pods and the ingress-labelled namespace, 7481 from the fleet's pods and the metrics-labelled namespace; postgres-dev/postgres.yaml admits 5432 only from the fleet's pods. Not applied to a cluster (record 94f64dc940).","supersedes":"f3620c4abb15438a738985bb05d5c9099f1fa32a754d02ce800226f4011f286a","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.599533Z","id":"04d803d3294ff4944e33bd7ee1f65b5aea3242b082fc240046e51898b5f42b12","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, The image, gives an overlay of one's own (remote resource, images, configMapGenerator with behavior: replace and disableNameSuffixHash) as the way to deploy.","supersedes":"5f8050199e39712a60422168a0250029cf65810daf82304e2444bd2199ef391f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772651Z","id":"c999ca9e4a1692cfab5d84503e7b8b825085975e69bc4933b812fa483423566b","body":{"detail":"networkpolicy.yaml:31-40 admits 7481 from namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/metrics=true \"for a Prometheus that scrapes /metrics\". Fleet::admin_router (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1083-1131) serves /metrics only when the peer is loopback or the request carries x-clax-fleet-key matching the peer key, so a scrape from another namespace gets 403. The only way to scrape is to give Prometheus CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY, which is the credential that makes a process believe a request's hop and instance headers (fleet.rs:22-25, proxy.rs:11): anyone who can read Prometheus's scrape config could then pass for a fleet pod. On PostgreSQL the key is kept in the database by default (main.rs:166-170), so an operator does not even have it. docs/kubernetes.md:264-277 and 344-347 describe /metrics without saying how it can be scraped.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1},"end":{"line":9},"content_hash":"2a63e22e2dae8c7b7eb9ea6abe08672b417c0a71897e56439a715482dc548307"},"suggested_fix":"Give /metrics its own credential (for example CLAX_FLEET_METRICS_TOKEN as a bearer token, or no auth since the NetworkPolicy already restricts 7481 and the metrics hold no tenant data), keep /status behind the peer key, and document the scrape config (a PodMonitor or prometheus.io annotations) in docs/kubernetes.md, Operating it.","summary":"The NetworkPolicy and docs invite a Prometheus to scrape /metrics, but /metrics answers 403 to anything but loopback or the peer key, and the peer key also lets its holder forge forwards","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772673Z","id":"65e7ec63c55a8f0ca68e5e5d9e2ae7306c80fc289d65f6ae9368c653057dc827","body":{"detail":"service.yaml:1-4 says clients reach the fleet through \"an Ingress, a load balancer\". networkpolicy.yaml admits 7480 only from the fleet's own pods and from namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/ingress=true; traffic that arrives through a cloud LoadBalancer or a NodePort comes from external or node addresses (with externalTrafficPolicy: Local the client's, otherwise a node's after SNAT), which neither peer selects, so on a CNI that enforces policy (Calico, Cilium) every request times out with nothing logged by the fleet. docs/kubernetes.md:264-269 only covers an Ingress controller.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":20},"end":{"line":30},"content_hash":"94efc357afbd705db9e65000cdf098f2f114efb24de818b8455daaee30535ffe"},"suggested_fix":"Say in service.yaml and docs/kubernetes.md, Security, that the policy assumes an in-cluster Ingress controller, and give the ipBlock rule (the load balancer's or nodes' CIDRs) to add for a LoadBalancer or NodePort front.","summary":"The base NetworkPolicy drops traffic from a LoadBalancer or NodePort Service, though service.yaml says clients may reach the fleet through a load balancer","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:14.036343Z","id":"77d253853b7a2e4f0e26771322bdd510c6a2f085f93db5022681709d1c7f6dc7","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml:5-7 and :31-40 admit 7481 from namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/metrics=true for a Prometheus. crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1085-1087 `allowed` is `peer.ip().to_canonical().is_loopback() || f.is_peer(headers)` and the /metrics route (fleet.rs:1113-1128) returns StatusCode::FORBIDDEN otherwise; is_peer (fleet.rs:329-334) compares KEY_HEADER with cfg.peer_key, the same key fleet.rs:22-25 says makes the hop and instance headers believed. main.rs:166-170 keeps the key in PostgreSQL when CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY is unset. One nuance: docs/kubernetes.md:275-277 does state that /status and /metrics answer only loopback or the peer key, but nowhere says how a Prometheus is to scrape, nor warns that giving it the key lets it forge forwards.","kind":"comment","references":"c999ca9e4a1692cfab5d84503e7b8b825085975e69bc4933b812fa483423566b","summary":"confirmed: /metrics is 403 to any non-loopback caller without the peer key, so the NetworkPolicy's Prometheus rule only works by handing Prometheus the forwarding credential","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:14.036588Z","id":"962b3b1f7b645f999089d70baf4ec9eb496429c742e25b6a0cd909876007494f","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml:20-30: the only 7480 rule's `from` is a podSelector for the fleet and a namespaceSelector clax.empathic.dev/ingress=true; policyTypes [Ingress] makes everything else denied. deploy/k8s/base/service.yaml:1 says clients reach the fleet through \"an Ingress, a load balancer\". Traffic arriving via a LoadBalancer or NodePort Service has an external or node source address, which neither peer (both pod/namespace selectors, no ipBlock) matches (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/). docs/kubernetes.md:264-269 covers only labelling the Ingress controller's namespace. A concern is the right kind: it bites only operators exposing the Service directly.","kind":"comment","references":"65e7ec63c55a8f0ca68e5e5d9e2ae7306c80fc289d65f6ae9368c653057dc827","summary":"confirmed: port 7480 admits only fleet pods and ingress-labelled namespaces, so LoadBalancer/NodePort traffic is dropped on an enforcing CNI","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:41.898090Z","id":"48c7a7a1b1dbe06a5631d12dc41ee22ada7a2a5fbb2bbd4a3b58fed8a54546d5","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"/metrics needs no credential, so a Prometheus the NetworkPolicy admits scrapes it without the peer key; docs/kubernetes.md documents the scrape (PodMonitor) and warns never to give Prometheus the peer key. Test: shards::the_admin_port_asks_for_the_key_for_status_alone. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"c999ca9e4a1692cfab5d84503e7b8b825085975e69bc4933b812fa483423566b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:41.981707Z","id":"d4a31504b01a82d65d3a99f953f4aabf3859d9715d4670766f7412f4e0fa3799","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"service.yaml and docs/kubernetes.md (Security) say the policy assumes an in-cluster Ingress controller; networkpolicy.yaml carries a commented ipBlock rule for a LoadBalancer or NodePort front. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"65e7ec63c55a8f0ca68e5e5d9e2ae7306c80fc289d65f6ae9368c653057dc827","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:42.721436Z","id":"f3f275e2ef8db3139e94c1cc6b33bb3d8ce93a8e115a9b12ff45d6178e2f353f","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"94f64dc940ff620c43e5ada05050870591c70a23e2a4c79e9d8282d31f8bb0c6","summary":"Rendered, not yet applied: `kubectl kustomize deploy/k8s/{sqlite,postgres,postgres-dev}` (kustomize v5.8.1) renders each overlay without error (7, 7 and 11 objects; postgres-dev includes the generated clax-postgres-dev Secret). No cluster was available to apply them, so admission, probes and the NetworkPolicies are still unchecked.","tags":["session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T04:34:54.979686Z","id":"0e0edc40db42df8de5a77056b7f7957578bb4132d0dd6aea07b12ab5fe3a1d60","body":{"detail":"No kubectl, kustomize or cluster was available: deploy/k8s/{base,sqlite,postgres,postgres-dev} were never built with kustomize nor applied. clax-fleet's tests check only that the variables they set are ones the binary reads and that base/fleet.toml parses (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs tests). Validate with `kubectl kustomize deploy/k8s/<overlay> | kubeconform -strict` and a kind cluster.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1},"end":{"line":24},"content_hash":"def328a31fd5686249d761cbea8cda3ce611376b29d7e071d44b3831c75b6e3b"},"summary":"The manifests have not been rendered or validated against a cluster","supersedes":"94f64dc940ff620c43e5ada05050870591c70a23e2a4c79e9d8282d31f8bb0c6","tags":["test-coverage","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
